WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.659 --> 00:00:04.140
Lee Jessup:&nbsp; And so she told she taught my
client to do the same thing.&nbsp; Fast forward

00:00:04.140 --> 00:00:09.860
20 years, my client is a comedy writer.&nbsp;
What does that tell you?&nbsp; Film Courage:&nbsp;

00:00:09.860 --> 00:00:13.780
I think this is another blog post of your’s
and I want to expand on it.&nbsp;Lee Jessup:&nbsp;

00:00:13.780 --> 00:00:18.830
Sure. Film Courage:&nbsp; So writers are great
at explaining someone else's story, but what

00:00:18.830 --> 00:00:23.651
about their own?&nbsp; Especially when it comes
to presenting themselves in the world, to

00:00:23.651 --> 00:00:30.480
the studio system.&nbsp; What is the refining
of their own story?&nbsp; What is too much [information]?&nbsp;

00:00:30.480 --> 00:00:33.770
How truthful should they be?&nbsp; Lee Jessup:&nbsp;
You want a writer to be truthful when they

00:00:33.770 --> 00:00:37.969
talk about themselves but you have to also
remember that a writer’s first story is

00:00:37.969 --> 00:00:41.969
their own.&nbsp; So if they don't know how to
tell their story it's going to be tough to

00:00:41.969 --> 00:00:47.910
trust them with other stories.&nbsp; So you really
have to figure out where is the ‘juice’

00:00:47.910 --> 00:00:52.809
in your story. &nbsp; It's never in chronology
or very rarely in chronology (sure if you

00:00:52.809 --> 00:00:59.570
travel to space, sure chronologies just fine).&nbsp;
But it is about finding a way to connect your

00:00:59.570 --> 00:01:03.639
human experience (for lack of better words)
to the person you're sitting across from you.&nbsp;

00:01:03.639 --> 00:01:07.460
To become interesting, to become unique, to
tell them a story they haven't heard that

00:01:07.460 --> 00:01:11.660
is truthful and your’s.&nbsp; And it can be
completely anecdotal.&nbsp; You know I’ve certainly

00:01:11.660 --> 00:01:15.870
talk to people who start out saying “I have
never done anything interesting in my life.”&nbsp;

00:01:15.870 --> 00:01:19.180
Well then, what’s your point of view on
the things that you've done?&nbsp; What have you

00:01:19.180 --> 00:01:25.230
made of your experiences to find those stories
that really speak to who you are as a person

00:01:25.230 --> 00:01:31.970
and as a writer.&nbsp; To tell something interesting
and unique so that a month later, a month

00:01:31.970 --> 00:01:37.090
after a general, be it with an executive in
a production company or studio, that executive

00:01:37.090 --> 00:01:41.220
looks at a piece of work and says “Hey what
happened to the writer who…the writer who

00:01:41.220 --> 00:01:46.460
had that experience so I can bring it into
this one?”&nbsp; So it is about sharing those

00:01:46.460 --> 00:01:52.451
experiences, those points of view and finding
a way to tell that personal story that is

00:01:52.451 --> 00:01:56.630
meaningful and that is connective and that
isn't tied up in a bow and doesn't have to

00:01:56.630 --> 00:02:01.730
be pretty.&nbsp; It has to be memorable and eloquent.&nbsp;
You have to be comfortable talking about it.&nbsp;

00:02:01.730 --> 00:02:06.780
So it can’t turn into a therapy session.&nbsp;
But it has to be that story that nobody else

00:02:06.780 --> 00:02:12.540
has to tell. &nbsp;So for a while everybody was
talking about writers who would go into networks

00:02:12.540 --> 00:02:17.430
and talk about how television was their only
friend. We've heard a lot of those stories.&nbsp;

00:02:17.430 --> 00:02:22.181
So those stories are no longer unique.&nbsp; Every
writer, every person has an experience and

00:02:22.181 --> 00:02:26.460
has a point of view is uniquely theirs. &nbsp; You
don't have to take half an hour to tell it.&nbsp;

00:02:26.460 --> 00:02:32.250
In fact we want it told in two minutes, three
minutes most.&nbsp; But relate to us something

00:02:32.250 --> 00:02:37.670
that we can remember that speaks to who you
are because that's who we want to work with.&nbsp;

00:02:37.670 --> 00:02:45.100
I have a writer who is a super talented, lovely,
lovely woman [who is] very, very shy who came

00:02:45.100 --> 00:02:49.740
to me and said “I don't know what my personal
story is and I can't really talk about it.”&nbsp;

00:02:49.740 --> 00:02:55.590
And so I had to bring in a lot of anecdotes.&nbsp;
There are a lot of different stories and they

00:02:55.590 --> 00:03:00.430
all came out kind of sad.&nbsp; The problem was
that she's a comedy writer. &nbsp; Until one day

00:03:00.430 --> 00:03:05.261
she brought me a story about her mother.&nbsp;
She's Korean and she grew up (naturally) with

00:03:05.261 --> 00:03:11.570
a Korean mother who was obsessed with not
wrinkling her face so she taught herself how

00:03:11.570 --> 00:03:18.371
to laugh without moving any of the muscles
on her face so that she would never age. &nbsp; And

00:03:18.371 --> 00:03:22.630
so she told she taught my client to do the
same thing.&nbsp; Fast forward 20 years, my client

00:03:22.630 --> 00:03:25.120
is a
comedy writer?&nbsp; What does that tell you?&nbsp;

00:03:25.120 --> 00:03:29.630
Of course she told it with a little bit more
flair and detail but those are the kind of

00:03:29.630 --> 00:03:36.421
stories that stick, that are unique, that
are memorable, that nobody else has. those

00:03:36.421 --> 00:03:40.290
are the stories that you want to share with
the industry because they'll remember you

00:03:40.290 --> 00:03:45.360
that way.&nbsp; Film Courage:&nbsp; Okay, so if someone
is envisioning their story and how they may

00:03:45.360 --> 00:03:51.250
explain it, first off how will they or in
what arena will they be telling the story.&nbsp;

00:03:51.250 --> 00:03:55.200
When would it ever come up to say “Hey you
know what, Lee?&nbsp; Tell me about yourself.&nbsp;

00:03:55.200 --> 00:03:57.860
I've seen what you've written but I want to
know your deal.”&nbsp; Lee Jessup: &nbsp; In every

00:03:57.860 --> 00:04:03.310
general meeting an executive will sit you
down and literally say “So tell me about

00:04:03.310 --> 00:04:09.209
yourself!” Just like that.&nbsp; We talked earlier
that I was doing a lot of interviews for this

00:04:09.209 --> 00:04:14.230
book that I just written and in that environment
I'd gone into me with agents, managers, executives

00:04:14.230 --> 00:04:19.940
that I've never met with before. I can't tell
you how many times folks that I was interviewing

00:04:19.940 --> 00:04:25.040
would sit down with me and say “So Lee,
tell me about yourself.” Those are the moments

00:04:25.040 --> 00:04:29.550
that are asked in every general.&nbsp; It’s
in every opportunity: Who are you?&nbsp; Is there

00:04:29.550 --> 00:04:33.661
anything for us to connect on?&nbsp; Is it the
story that you tell? Is it the way that you

00:04:33.661 --> 00:04:39.030
tell that story?&nbsp; Is it the sensibility with
which you see the world?&nbsp; So it's in every

00:04:39.030 --> 00:04:44.740
general meeting. Every last general meeting
“Tell me about yourself.” Other than “What

00:04:44.740 --> 00:04:48.760
else do you have?” it’s perhaps the most
popular question in this industry.&nbsp; Film

00:04:48.760 --> 00:04:55.310
Courage:&nbsp; Lee where would someone actually
be telling their story and how deep do they

00:04:55.310 --> 00:05:00.440
go?&nbsp; How light do they keep it?&nbsp; Can I envision
the scenario as to how someone's going to

00:05:00.440 --> 00:05:04.430
be asking about me?&nbsp; Lee Jessup:&nbsp; Sure.&nbsp;
Oftentimes it's going to happen at a general

00:05:04.430 --> 00:05:09.430
meeting it's niceties, over a bottle of water
“Tell me about yourself.” “Can I get

00:05:09.430 --> 00:05:15.500
you something to drink? Tell me about yourself.”
It's very blunt and upfront and “do your

00:05:15.500 --> 00:05:21.350
show.”&nbsp; You know I have writers who have
different versions of their stories. So I

00:05:21.350 --> 00:05:26.330
have one writer who suffered a traumatic event
in her childhood and if she is meeting with

00:05:26.330 --> 00:05:30.910
somebody that she feels an instant bond and
trust with then she will go deeper, she will

00:05:30.910 --> 00:05:37.360
go darker. With others she will leave the
nitty-gritty out. So you tailor your story

00:05:37.360 --> 00:05:42.341
a little bit to see what and how dark you
want to go.&nbsp; But the rule is never make it

00:05:42.341 --> 00:05:46.639
a therapy session. Never bring in a story
that you're not ready to talk about openly

00:05:46.639 --> 00:05:53.280
without breaking down without having a moment
of tears.&nbsp; All of that is unnecessary because

00:05:53.280 --> 00:05:57.070
a lot of (specifically in TV) TV really looks
at what do you bring into the [writer’s]

00:05:57.070 --> 00:06:01.120
room, right? It's not just what you’re writing
is but what personal experience, what themes

00:06:01.120 --> 00:06:06.090
speak to you? What do you bring with you into
the writer’s room so they want to know that

00:06:06.090 --> 00:06:10.650
you are capable of talking about whatever
it is you're bringing comfortably. That it's

00:06:10.650 --> 00:06:14.020
not going to be a therapy session. That there's
not going to be suddenly an elephant in the

00:06:14.020 --> 00:06:19.440
room that makes everybody uncomfortable. So
you want to have those layers to the story.

00:06:19.440 --> 00:06:25.100
Comedy writers tend to really look for the
lighter, fluffier, happier stuff or the really

00:06:25.100 --> 00:06:32.889
dark stuff told in a very funny way, you know.
Tragedy via comedy. The drama writers tend

00:06:32.889 --> 00:06:39.130
to have those more layered stories that they
choose how deeply to go into.&nbsp; So if it's

00:06:39.130 --> 00:06:45.800
being abandoned by your father and the life
of petty crime you're forced into at 12 and

00:06:45.800 --> 00:06:50.550
how cute you were getting away with it? Or
was it the nights that you spent alone and

00:06:50.550 --> 00:06:55.419
when you didn't have money to pay for electricity
and what that felt like when social services

00:06:55.419 --> 00:06:59.180
showed up and tried to uproot you from your
home.&nbsp; Those are different ways to tell the

00:06:59.180 --> 00:07:03.600
same story. So writers really do pick and
choose and it's important that you assess

00:07:03.600 --> 00:07:09.090
your environment to really determine which
version of which story you're telling. Ultimately

00:07:09.090 --> 00:07:12.870
it's always going to be the same story because
you're going to get known for that story in

00:07:12.870 --> 00:07:17.170
them they'll be other anecdotes that will
come along with it but it really is about

00:07:17.170 --> 00:07:21.820
finding that core story and taking it out
there and getting the industry to know you

00:07:21.820 --> 00:07:25.060
through it. Film Courage: Okay so different
versions.&nbsp; Lee Jessup:&nbsp; Yeah.&nbsp;Film Courage:&nbsp;

00:07:25.060 --> 00:07:31.680
Maybe have an a, b and c version.&nbsp; Lee Jessup:&nbsp;
Yeah you have a kind of deep version where

00:07:31.680 --> 00:07:37.070
you really kind of drill into the story and
then you have your (more for lack of better

00:07:37.070 --> 00:07:41.919
words) shallow version of it that is less
confrontational, less demanding of the listener,

00:07:41.919 --> 00:07:47.270
less uncomfortable potentially.&nbsp; It really
is how you look at it. Some writers prefer

00:07:47.270 --> 00:07:51.669
to not expose any sort of wound when they
talk about a personal story. They're perfectly

00:07:51.669 --> 00:07:57.050
happy to stay surfacing and just allude to
it. But it really is about planning and rehearsing

00:07:57.050 --> 00:08:03.199
it and exploring it so that when you go into
a room that's not when you're excavating your

00:08:03.199 --> 00:08:05.639
own story and figuring out what's what works.

